COMMIT 27d4b9bf8d07461c8d65fd017d32f37928982688
test(core): pin the orphaned flush window that fires early, and say so in flush()

D-104 accepts that a flush() whose cancel() throws leaves the dropped window
SCHEDULED, so it can fire later and hand a newer partial batch to the sink
before that batch's own window has elapsed -- a loss of batching, never of
data. Nothing asserted that, so the opposite could have become true in
silence. This adds the one case that pins it, beside the twin that already
covered the non-throwing path, and the half-sentence flush()'s javadoc was
missing. No production behaviour changes.

The case is green the moment it is written, so its red had to come from
somewhere honest: it must fail when the throwing cancel() is disabled. Runs 1
and 2 failed on fixture checks rather than on behaviour, so the test-author
stripped those in a temporary copy until the behavioural line itself failed --
expected <[[a], [b], [c], [d]]> but was <[[a], [b], [c]]> -- then restored the
file byte for byte. That went past its brief and was the right call; the
reviewer confirmed from the diff that none of it survived.

Both review FAILs were on the architect's wording, not on the agents' work.
The first draft said "never of order" unqualified, while :209-212 of the same
class says no caller may rely on the order batches reach the sink. The
replacement contradicted itself: if whole batches may arrive in any order,
then items from different batches do too. What the code actually gives is the
order items are TAKEN from the queue -- queue.poll() at the head under the
lock, each batch a contiguous in-order slice -- and that is what the javadoc
now claims and no more.

Per the reviewer, the case earns its place by catching a change that would
make the orphan harmless, such as a generation or identity guard in onWindow
or flush; the twin stays green for those. It does not catch dropping
`armed = null`, which the twin already covers.

FIX-136's packet gains the note on where its new NACK-text assertion gets its
red, since that assertion cannot be red until the catch is widened.

